/* Gaussian elimination keeps its working arrays in special variables
   and passes them around by name: SOLVEX builds the coefficient matrix
   in the value of XA*, the matrix functions build theirs in *MAT*, the
   Risch integrator's LSA in *JM*, and TFGELI1 keeps its row and column
   permutations in *ROW*, *COL* and *COLINV*.  Each must be bound by the
   call that owns it.  Assigned globally, two runners eliminate in each
   other's arrays: measured on SBCL, 48 parallel solve() calls of systems
   with a unique solution failed with a Lisp error in 5 runs of 5, and
   with only XA* bound 3 runs of 5 returned wrong solutions with no error
   at all (GitHub issue #58).

   These first checks need no threads.  Each operation must leave the
   global value of all six variables exactly as it found it -- bound to
   the same object, or unbound -- which is what keeps concurrent calls
   out of each other's way. */
(kill(all),
 scratch_of(l) :=
   map(lambda([s], if ?boundp(s) then [?symbol\-value(s)] else []), l),
 elimination_scratch() :=
   scratch_of(['?xa\*, '?\*row\*, '?\*col\*, '?\*colinv\*, '?\*mat\*,
               '?\*jm\*]),
 same_scratch(a, b) :=
   every(lambda([p, q],
                if p = [] then is(q = [])
                else is(q # [] and ?eq(first(p), first(q)))),
         a, b),
 0);

0$

(s0: elimination_scratch(),
 [solve([x + y = 3, x - y = 1], [x, y]),
  same_scratch(s0, elimination_scratch())]);

[[[x = 2, y = 1]], true]$

(s0: elimination_scratch(),
 [linsolve([x + y + z = 6, x - y = 1, x + z = 4], [x, y, z]),
  same_scratch(s0, elimination_scratch())]);

[[x = 3, y = 2, z = 1], true]$

(s0: elimination_scratch(),
 [block([ratmx: true], determinant(matrix([2, 1, 0], [1, 3, 1], [0, 1, 4]))),
  same_scratch(s0, elimination_scratch())]);

[18, true]$

/* The sparse determinant keeps its matrix in *MAT* too.  Only that is
   checked here: its own routines still assign *ROW* and *COL* (through
   TMLATTICE in linnew.lisp) and other globals of their own, which this
   change leaves alone.  It also fails on larger matrices (issue #62);
   this one is within what it gets right. */
(s0: scratch_of(['?\*mat\*]),
 [block([sparse: true], determinant(matrix([1, 2], [3, 4]))),
  same_scratch(s0, scratch_of(['?\*mat\*]))]);

[-2, true]$

(s0: elimination_scratch(),
 [rank(matrix([1, 2, 3], [2, 4, 6], [1, 0, 1])),
  same_scratch(s0, elimination_scratch())]);

[2, true]$

